# 3. ARCHITECTURE

```
Browser (Vite SPA, Render Static Site)
  ├─ Firebase Auth (Google + Email/Password + email verification)  → ID token
  ├─ Firestore (client SDK, rules-protected): realtime reads of the user's own plans/meals/grocery
  └─ HTTPS + Bearer ID token → Render Web Service "api" (Express)
        ├─ verifies token (Admin SDK), requires email_verified, optional App Check
        ├─ POST /v1/plans → validates → enqueues job → 202 {planId}
        ├─ rate limits + quotas (Redis), idempotency keys
        └─ reads/writes Firestore (Admin SDK)

Render Background Worker "worker" (same codebase, entry src/worker.ts) via BullMQ on Render Key Value (Redis)
  ├─ job plan.generate: load library → fill gaps with Gemini → solve → write plan/meals/grocery → progress updates
  ├─ job recipe.generate: Gemini structured recipe(s) → validate → save to library
  └─ job image.generate: Gemini image → sharp → Firebase Storage → update recipe doc
```

Rules:
- The API and worker are **stateless** and horizontally scalable. No in-process state that matters. Redis holds the queue, rate limiter, and short-lived caches.
- Plan generation is **asynchronous**. The client creates the plan, then subscribes to the Firestore plan doc with `onSnapshot` to show live progress ("Choosing dishes… Pricing your market… Cooking up photos… Done").
- If Redis is missing (local dev), fall back to an in-memory queue and limiter so `npm run dev` works with zero infrastructure besides Firebase emulators or a dev project.
- If Gemini is down, rate-limited, or the daily AI spend cap is reached, **degrade gracefully**: build the plan from the existing library only and show a notice. Never fail the whole request because an image or a new recipe could not be generated.

