## 6.3 Bento layout

- 12-column CSS Grid, `gap:16px` (12px on mobile), container max 1240px. `BentoTile` takes `colSpan`, `rowSpan` and `tone` (`paper | card | clay | moss | turmeric | ink`) props mapped to CSS classes, no inline styles.
- Mix tile sizes deliberately (for example 5×2, 4×1, 3×1, 3×2, 4×2) so the grid has rhythm. Never a uniform row of identical cards.
- Responsive: 12 columns on desktop, 6 on tablet, 1 on mobile, with an intentional mobile order (headline tile, key number tile, imagery, details).
- Each tile has one job: a single number, a single image, a single idea. Generous internal padding (24 to 32px), a small mono eyebrow, then content.
- Organic touches: hand-drawn-feeling SVG dividers, blob-shaped image masks (SVG `clip-path`), subtle duotone image treatments, small leaf/grain/steam motifs. All original SVG, no stock art.

## 6.4 Motion (CSS first; `motion` only for layout, route transitions and number counters)

Easing `cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1)`. Durations: 160ms (micro), 280ms (UI), 600ms (reveal). Animate only `transform` and `opacity`.
- Scroll-reveal with 60ms stagger (IntersectionObserver; use CSS `animation-timeline: view()` as progressive enhancement).
- Hero: budget ring draws from 0 to its value; the money figure counts up; grocery list ticks check themselves one by one; dish tiles drift 4 to 8px on slow parallax. Pointer tilt max 3° only on `(pointer:fine)`.
- Tiles lift 2px with hairline darkening on hover. Buttons have a pressed state (scale .98).
- Meal swap: outgoing card slides and fades, the new card rises in. A total "settles" with a count animation.
- Checkbox "ink draw" for purchased items. Warm-tinted skeleton shimmer. Route transitions: crossfade plus 12px rise.
- Slow marquee of ingredient names on the landing page. Pause on hover.
- `prefers-reduced-motion`: replace all motion with opacity fades under 120ms and disable parallax, tilt and marquee.

## 6.4b Icons and imagery
Phosphor Light/Duotone at consistent sizes (16/20/24) in `currentColor`. **Never emoji as icons.** Original SVG mark and wordmark (a simple bowl glyph with a leaf-shaped steam curl, wordmark in Young Serif). Illustrations are original organic-shape SVGs. Photos are the generated dish images.

